WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.527 --> 00:00:05.660
For over 100 years, Hertz has been

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:07.197
one of the most recognizable names

00:00:07.197 --> 00:00:09.400
in the car rental business.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:13.661
♪ Let Hertz put you in the driver's seat ♪

00:00:13.661 --> 00:00:15.160
Nobody has more of what it takes

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:19.242
to get you into a new LTD
or other fine car faster.

00:00:19.242 --> 00:00:22.242
♪ Hertz's superstar ♪

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:26.690
But just like their
superstar spokesperson,

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:29.113
Hertz wold eventually
come to a complete halt.

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:34.620
The short version is Corona virus hits,

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:38.224
nobody's traveling, and
they went into bankruptcy.

00:00:38.224 --> 00:00:41.470
The long version is, they
were as badly prepared

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:44.220
for something like this as
a company could possibly be.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:47.550
It's almost every step of the way,

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:51.130
if you were to say here's
what not to do for a company,

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:54.840
it almost feels like
Hertz did exactly that.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:58.010
It's just sort of one
flameout after another,

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.750
one missed step after another.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:05.203
Did they not understand how
fragile this company was?

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:07.380
This is the story of how

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:10.520
a company prioritized
shareholders over stability

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:12.973
and paid the price once catastrophe hit.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:23.270
Coincidentally, the company
that would become Hertz

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:25.940
was started in the middle
of another pandemic.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:29.060
In 1918, during the
Spanish Influenza outbreak,

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:30.840
a businessman named Walter Jacobs

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.330
started a car rental business in Chicago

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:35.860
with just 12 Ford Model Ts.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:39.372
Six years later, the
company grew to 600 cars.

00:01:39.372 --> 00:01:43.250
That's when John Hertz, then
head of the Yellow Cab Company,

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:45.072
would be the first to acquire the company

00:01:45.072 --> 00:01:48.420
and rename it to the
Hertz DRIV-UR-SELF System.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.880
After that, Hertz's ownership
would exchange hands

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.800
quite a lot, from companies
like General Motors,

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:56.060
RCA, and eventually Ford.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:59.300
But during this time, the
company saw a ton of growth.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:04.300
The rise of Hertz in, in the
1930s, 40s, 50s, and beyond

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:08.520
coincides with the era of
increased work traveling,

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:11.150
plane traveling, across the world.

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:12.960
Hertz, for example, in the early days,

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.760
it's able to put one of its
car lots at a Chicago airport,

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:18.210
so that's really helpful for them.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:21.760
They move into this area
where people are going

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.780
to be getting off of
planes and immediately want

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:25.930
to rent a car to drive to the hotel,

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:27.400
drive to their business meeting.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:31.293
And they're also able to
expand internationally as well.

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:35.980
It really kind of became
the name in rental cars.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:37.420
They really were the first company

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:39.390
to grow big time doing this.

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:41.320
And they were very popular
with business customers,

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:44.370
so it was seen as aspirational
brand for the guy getting

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:46.450
off the plane in his business
suit with a briefcase

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:49.950
and hopping into a car after
he left the Hertz counter.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:53.250
I know it's a commodity business,
but it's an iconic brand.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:54.810
In the 1970s, Hertz would look

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:56.850
to advertise their speed of service.

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:58.110
And in order to do this,

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:01.019
they turned to a professional
athlete who, at the time,

00:03:01.019 --> 00:03:02.450
embodied speed.

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:04.107
They're giving him the chance!

00:03:04.107 --> 00:03:05.683
Now there he goes!

00:03:05.683 --> 00:03:09.212
O.J. Simpson has broken what seemed

00:03:09.212 --> 00:03:11.770
to be an insurmountable barrier!

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:15.000
At the time, O.J. was an American hero.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.630
He was this great form of football player.

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:18.630
He was an actor.

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:20.344
He was in some of the "Naked Gun" movies.

00:03:20.344 --> 00:03:21.440
Nordberg!

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:22.273
Hiya, buddy!

00:03:22.273 --> 00:03:23.106
Hey!

00:03:23.106 --> 00:03:23.971
Doc says I should be on my feet

00:03:23.971 --> 00:03:25.580
and as good as new in a week!

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:27.600
People absolutely loved the guy.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:32.095
Hertz kind of got him at
the peak of his popularity.

00:03:32.095 --> 00:03:33.540
Others claim to be fast,

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:35.810
but nobody has more to do it faster,

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:38.710
more pros to execute the
toughest performance standards.

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:40.560
Hertz's relationship with O.J. lasted

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.160
from 1975 to the mid-90s.

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:44.450
But while the company would continue

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:46.880
to advertise their speed and efficiency,

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:48.500
they would have new competition.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:51.030
Enterprise, a privately-owned
car rental company,

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:53.300
known more for their
locations within cities,

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:55.530
began expanding into airports.

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:58.960
The whole Hertz-O.J. connection,
much like O.J.'s life,

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:03.960
had this ignominious end where
the same year that he was

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:08.840
in the highway chase in this
white Ford Bronco, trying

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:11.230
to evade the police and figure
out what he's going to do

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:13.100
while he's being chased in
connection with the death

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:14.750
of Nicole Brown Simpson,

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:18.433
Hertz also lost their top
position for, for market share

00:04:18.433 --> 00:04:20.410
in the US to Enterprise.

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:22.765
So O.J.'s no longer this American hero.

00:04:22.765 --> 00:04:25.590
Hertz is no longer the rental king.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:27.340
And it all kind of happens all at once.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:29.350
And the down slide for the company,

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:31.413
sort of in earnest, started right there.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:34.720
Hertz may have lost the
crown to Enterprise,

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:38.090
but by the mid-2000s, it was
still bring in tidy profits

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:40.240
for their parent company, Ford.

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:43.580
Ford was a company in trouble
to please its shareholders.

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:46.790
It really wanted to focus
in on its core business,

00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:48.412
which is of course cars.

00:04:48.412 --> 00:04:51.010
And so then you get Ford selling Hertz

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:52.868
to this private equity group.

00:04:52.868 --> 00:04:55.600
In 2005, the company
would again change hands

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:57.040
to two private equity firms,

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:59.250
and Merrill Lynch and Co's buy-out unit,

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:01.203
for about $15 billion.

00:05:01.203 --> 00:05:04.300
But losing Ford as an
owner had a big impact

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:06.262
on a core part of Hertz's business,

00:05:06.262 --> 00:05:08.410
where they got their vehicles.

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:10.100
They basically churn automobiles.

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:11.190
They buy cars.

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:12.750
They rent them for some period of time,

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:13.930
three months, six months.

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:14.763
They make money off of that.

00:05:14.763 --> 00:05:17.140
And then they hope to sell them

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:20.770
for more than the resale
value as in the market.

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:21.820
While owned by Ford,

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:24.210
Hertz would receive excess
cars from its parent company

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:25.860
at a considerable discount.

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:28.280
Many of these cars were
known as program cars,

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:30.430
where the manufacturer would
agree to buy back the car

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:32.526
at a certain rate of depreciation.

00:05:32.526 --> 00:05:34.923
But once they were no
longer part of the company,

00:05:34.923 --> 00:05:38.320
Ford would often demand a
higher price for each vehicle.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:39.570
We were then forced

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:42.428
to buy cars under what
was called our own risk.

00:05:42.428 --> 00:05:47.290
And that completely changed
the nature of fleet management

00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:50.320
and the profit model in
the rental car industry.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:51.520
This is Maryann Keller.

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:53.250
She's an automotive industry analyst

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:55.550
and a former board member
for Dollar Thrifty,

00:05:55.550 --> 00:05:57.760
another car rental company
that will become important

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:00.340
a little later on in Hertz's story.

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:03.640
Suddenly, you really had
to buy cars carefully,

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:05.330
even to the color of the car.

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:08.510
Because you were really looking
at not just what you paid

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:12.520
for the car, but what you were
going to realize on that car

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:14.279
when it came out of service.

00:06:14.279 --> 00:06:17.000
The new private equity
firm would also kick off

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:19.910
another, ultimately fatal trend at Hertz,

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:21.502
taking on debt.

00:06:21.502 --> 00:06:24.610
The Hertz transaction
was valued at 15 billion.

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:27.440
But only 5.6 billion of
that was paid in cash,

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:29.697
the rest was paid by
saddling Hertz up with debt,

00:06:29.697 --> 00:06:32.520
a trend that, over the
next decade-and-a-half,

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:33.916
would continue.

00:06:33.916 --> 00:06:37.224
One of the first things this
private equity group did

00:06:37.224 --> 00:06:41.510
was take out a $1 billion dividend.

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:44.480
And what that means is that
they're able to use the returns

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:47.100
that are able to
shareholders and make it look

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:49.230
like there're more
profits coming from Hertz,

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:51.990
make it a more viable investment,

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:54.200
and it also allows them to take on debt

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:56.670
to do more mergers and acquisitions.

00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:58.290
To navigate the company under this new,

00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:01.170
private equity ownership,
Hertz hired Mark Frissora

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:02.639
to be the new CEO.

00:07:02.639 --> 00:07:04.648
Frissora is a pretty charismatic guy.

00:07:04.648 --> 00:07:06.839
He was an acquirer. He was a negotiator.

00:07:06.839 --> 00:07:09.650
He's a bit of a character.
He's also a deal guy.

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:11.100
Frissora would go on

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:13.260
to have many successful
years managing Hertz,

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:14.712
including through the financial crisis

00:07:14.712 --> 00:07:18.750
where Hertz's stock would
fall as low as $5.35 a share

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:20.350
before recovering to a high point

00:07:20.350 --> 00:07:23.429
of over $100 a share under Frissora.

00:07:23.429 --> 00:07:26.210
The stock price went up
during Frissora's time

00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:28.910
because of what, what, honestly,

00:07:28.910 --> 00:07:30.827
we would call over promising

00:07:30.827 --> 00:07:33.300
and essentially under delivering.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:36.709
But the under delivering side,
the market found out later.

00:07:36.709 --> 00:07:39.860
At the time, it was just big promises

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:41.942
that the market believed.

00:07:41.942 --> 00:07:44.880
So he's very, very focused on

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:46.340
the financials of the company

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:48.620
because this is what the
shareholders care about.

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:52.360
But Mark Frissora, over
his tenure of Hertz,

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:54.180
makes a lot of mistakes.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:57.412
There was significant
underinvestment in the business.

00:07:57.412 --> 00:08:02.412
And what we mean by that is
that there was underinvestment

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:03.640
in the fleet.

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:06.913
Usually car rental companies
will refresh their fleet

00:08:06.913 --> 00:08:09.200
almost every year.

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:12.670
And in Mark Frissora's time,
there was a year or two

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:15.770
where they just delayed
refreshing their fleet.

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:20.213
And what that did was that it
lowered the depreciation rate

00:08:20.213 --> 00:08:23.886
in their PNL, which means
that they were over earning

00:08:23.886 --> 00:08:28.330
in order to hit certain
Wall Street targets.

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:30.630
Such accounting practices
would prompt the SEC

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:33.890
to file complaints against
Hertz and Frissora.

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:36.170
Because the first year that you own a car,

00:08:36.170 --> 00:08:38.231
that's when you see the
biggest depreciation.

00:08:38.231 --> 00:08:41.729
And that depreciation, that
lowered value in the car,

00:08:41.729 --> 00:08:45.020
you have to put that into
your financials as a cost.

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:48.700
So it looks bad, so instead
of buying a bunch of new cars,

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:51.000
he just keeps the cars for longer

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:52.628
so he doesn't have that cost.

00:08:52.628 --> 00:08:56.930
The SEC complaint against him
also said that he had asked

00:08:56.930 --> 00:08:59.890
some of his employees to make money appear

00:08:59.890 --> 00:09:02.940
when it looked like his
financials would miss estimates

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:04.460
for the various earnings.

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:06.060
Hertz and Frissora would eventually settle

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:09.133
with the SEC without admitting
or denying any wrongdoing.

00:09:09.133 --> 00:09:11.410
Hertz declined comment for the story

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:14.260
and Frissora didn't return
a request for comment.

00:09:14.260 --> 00:09:17.057
A spokesperson for the former
CEO said previously that,

00:09:17.057 --> 00:09:20.230
"Hertz's 2015 earnings
restatements have no bearing

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:22.065
on the company's current
financial situation,

00:09:22.065 --> 00:09:25.600
and that Frissora didn't
direct any improper accounting

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:27.955
or engage in any wrongdoing."

00:09:27.955 --> 00:09:31.070
But the decision to keep
cars in the fleet longer

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:34.380
did have an effect on its core customers.

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:37.300
Given the fact that hertz
was the dominant player

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:39.640
in the corporate travel market,

00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:42.020
that was a huge risk.

00:09:42.020 --> 00:09:45.050
Because you don't put
a traveling executive

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:48.810
in a car with 50,000 miles on
it that squeaks and smells.

00:09:48.810 --> 00:09:51.280
You just don't. And that's what he did.

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:53.687
The other thing that
happened during Frissora

00:09:53.687 --> 00:09:57.630
was they moved the headquarters
from New Jersey to Florida.

00:09:57.630 --> 00:10:01.000
And 85% of the staff, the corporate staff,

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:03.090
didn't show up, meaning
that they didn't move

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:04.080
with the company.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:06.891
They had to rehire a lot of people.

00:10:06.891 --> 00:10:08.980
Hertz would find itself
carrying a lot of debt

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:11.210
at this point, and a large
portion of that coming

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:14.767
from its acquisition of the car
rental brand Dollar Thrifty.

00:10:14.767 --> 00:10:19.410
Now, he wanted to buy Dollar
Thrifty for most of the time

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:20.720
that I was director.

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:25.720
It started when we had
financial troubles in 2007-8,

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:28.140
when there was a collapse
in the used-car market,

00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:30.152
there was a collapse in the economy.

00:10:30.152 --> 00:10:34.260
But negotiations didn't
go well for Frissora.

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:37.603
It was chaos. It was chaos.

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:41.770
He had an opportunity to buy the company

00:10:41.770 --> 00:10:44.740
at, I think, $54 a share.

00:10:44.740 --> 00:10:46.260
We had been telling him

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:48.390
that unless he would increase the price,

00:10:48.390 --> 00:10:49.600
he would not buy it.

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:51.670
He refused to listen to us.

00:10:51.670 --> 00:10:53.700
Hertz's initial offer was rejected.

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:56.620
And it kicked off a bidding
war with Avis Rent-A-Car.

00:10:56.620 --> 00:10:58.100
At the same time, Dollar
Thrifty was able to

00:10:58.100 --> 00:11:00.770
successfully navigate the recession,

00:11:00.770 --> 00:11:02.550
helping drive up its own price.

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:05.570
We knew that we were the
last bite at the apple.

00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:09.120
We knew that we were running as profitably

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:11.220
as anybody in the business.

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:13.400
He could have had the
company for something like,

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:15.830
I think, $54 a share.

00:11:15.830 --> 00:11:18.130
And he eventually paid 87.50.

00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:21.060
We were very profitable, very profitable.

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:24.060
Ultimately, Hertz would spend $2.6 billion

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:25.190
on the acquisition.

00:11:25.190 --> 00:11:28.150
However, it would quickly
also run into costly issues

00:11:28.150 --> 00:11:29.190
with integration.

00:11:29.190 --> 00:11:32.000
They had a really hard time
integrating the two companies.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:34.560
Things like they had two
different computer systems.

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:36.360
They couldn't get the
computer systems to talk

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:37.190
to each other.

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:38.770
If you're doing a buyout of a company,

00:11:38.770 --> 00:11:40.030
if you're merging two companies,

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:41.890
this is a very simple thing,

00:11:41.890 --> 00:11:43.340
it usually isn't a big headache,

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:45.600
but in this case it was.

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:46.580
According to two people

00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:47.680
familiar with the matter,

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:49.420
the merger that was supposed to save Hertz

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:51.790
100 million a year ended up costing them

00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:54.000
70 million in the first year.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:56.120
And, in 2014, the
company would find itself

00:11:56.120 --> 00:11:59.330
with $16 billion worth of debt.

00:11:59.330 --> 00:12:02.020
And what bothered me, obviously,

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:04.750
was the fact that we were being blamed for

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:08.360
what was likely to happen
in terms of their earnings.

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:10.480
And so I spoke to a friend of mine

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:13.170
who had contacts in
the Icahn organization.

00:12:13.170 --> 00:12:15.170
And I said, do you think we
should have dinner with him

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:17.610
and just raise this subject with him?

00:12:17.610 --> 00:12:21.590
Carl Icahn is very well known
as this activist investor.

00:12:21.590 --> 00:12:24.350
So when he likes a company, he'll come in

00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:26.390
and change it for the better,

00:12:26.390 --> 00:12:28.280
and make their stock price rise.

00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:29.510
And that's how he makes his money

00:12:29.510 --> 00:12:31.200
when he does things like this.

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:34.430
Carl Icahn has purchased roughly

00:12:34.430 --> 00:12:38.240
an 8.48% stake in Hertz.

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:40.300
Carl Icahn pops up with a filing

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:41.870
saying he's taken a large stake

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:43.680
and seeking to make
changes at the companies.

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:45.210
There's a lot going on there.

00:12:45.210 --> 00:12:48.130
This is a classic acquisition for Icahn.

00:12:48.130 --> 00:12:51.980
It's a company that has a
great brand, it's well-known,

00:12:51.980 --> 00:12:53.530
but kind of fallen on hard times.

00:12:53.530 --> 00:12:56.840
And he thinks he can
come in and fix it up.

00:12:56.840 --> 00:12:59.170
He essentially forced Frissora out.

00:12:59.170 --> 00:13:02.720
Hertz CEO and Chairman Mark Frissora

00:13:02.720 --> 00:13:05.960
is stepping down from the company.

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:06.790
Rather than hire

00:13:06.790 --> 00:13:09.240
the former Dollar Thrifty
CEO, Scott Thompson,

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:12.020
to run the company, Icahn
went with John Tague,

00:13:12.020 --> 00:13:14.550
an ex COO of United Airlines.

00:13:14.550 --> 00:13:18.010
Tague definitely inherited
a company in a mess.

00:13:18.010 --> 00:13:23.010
He has to deal with the SEC
accusations against Hertz.

00:13:23.350 --> 00:13:26.250
He has to deal with
integrating the two companies.

00:13:26.250 --> 00:13:30.040
And part of what he tried to
do was to update the fleet,

00:13:30.040 --> 00:13:32.720
because, again, the
strategy of Hertz had been

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:35.020
to let some of these cars get old.

00:13:35.020 --> 00:13:37.120
Hertz would find new,
even more creative ways

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:38.480
to take on debt.

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:41.110
Often leasing cars through
a subsidiary company

00:13:41.110 --> 00:13:42.960
as asset backed securities.

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:45.400
But Hertz would also end
up buying cheaper sedans

00:13:45.400 --> 00:13:47.470
rather than the more popular SUVs,

00:13:47.470 --> 00:13:49.240
and their customers noticed.

00:13:49.240 --> 00:13:51.510
By 2017, John Tague would retire

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:54.050
and new CEO Kathryn
Marinello would inherent

00:13:54.050 --> 00:13:56.760
13.5 billion in debt.

00:13:56.760 --> 00:13:59.020
When Marinello takes over
she has a lot of problems

00:13:59.020 --> 00:13:59.850
to deal with.

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:01.830
She's got this fleet of
cars that people don't want.

00:14:01.830 --> 00:14:04.040
And she's got an old reservation system

00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:05.890
that wasn't working well.

00:14:05.890 --> 00:14:07.260
So she spends a lot of money

00:14:07.260 --> 00:14:09.710
in a very short amount of
time to fix that system

00:14:09.710 --> 00:14:12.620
and to upgrade the fleet of cars there,

00:14:12.620 --> 00:14:14.620
and offer people what they really want.

00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:17.120
She is doing the right things.

00:14:17.120 --> 00:14:18.880
She's helping to reduce some of the debt.

00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:21.470
She's getting customers back to Hertz.

00:14:21.470 --> 00:14:23.870
The cars are starting to look better,

00:14:23.870 --> 00:14:25.980
but there's a question of

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:29.870
could she ever have fixed
Hertz in the state it was in

00:14:29.870 --> 00:14:31.890
or did it just require more time?

00:14:31.890 --> 00:14:34.170
But Hertz never got that time.

00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:37.300
The deadly coronavirus,
officially hitting the US.

00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:39.520
As the world grapples
with the deadly outbreak,

00:14:39.520 --> 00:14:42.360
people have grown anxious
about being in crowds

00:14:42.360 --> 00:14:45.170
or traveling in confined
spaces like airplanes.

00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:49.060
So the pandemic hits and
all the rental car companies

00:14:49.060 --> 00:14:53.580
have these lots at
airports and on main drags

00:14:53.580 --> 00:14:56.260
all over the country,
just packed with cars.

00:14:56.260 --> 00:14:58.800
This is a big problem in
a rental car business,

00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:01.290
you just can't get rid of
vehicles really quickly.

00:15:01.290 --> 00:15:02.870
So we have nobody renting the cars,

00:15:02.870 --> 00:15:04.030
so money isn't coming in.

00:15:04.030 --> 00:15:06.170
And then you have to go
to these bond holders,

00:15:06.170 --> 00:15:09.080
who want to be paid 100% in full.

00:15:09.080 --> 00:15:11.620
So that gets them in a
place where they eventually

00:15:11.620 --> 00:15:12.940
have to declare bankruptcy,

00:15:12.940 --> 00:15:16.420
because they just didn't
have enough cash on hand

00:15:16.420 --> 00:15:19.770
to pay back the people who
lent them all that debt.

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:21.510
Hertz's Chapter 11 petition

00:15:21.510 --> 00:15:25.160
would show the company would
have over $24 billion in debt

00:15:25.160 --> 00:15:27.560
and only a billion of cash on hand.

00:15:27.560 --> 00:15:29.820
Kathryn Marinello would resign as CEO

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:32.050
a week before filing bankruptcy.

00:15:32.050 --> 00:15:34.000
And Carl Icahn would eventually sell

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:35.910
his entire stock in the company,

00:15:35.910 --> 00:15:39.120
losing an estimated $1.6 billion.

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:42.020
Carl's got a lot of money,
he's a legitimate billionaire,

00:15:42.020 --> 00:15:45.290
but even for him that
was a catastrophic loss.

00:15:45.290 --> 00:15:48.300
It does seem like Hertz can survive this.

00:15:48.300 --> 00:15:50.340
Maybe it will be a much smaller company

00:15:50.340 --> 00:15:51.940
when it does survive this,

00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:55.490
maybe the structure of Hertz,
and the way its debt looks,

00:15:55.490 --> 00:15:59.630
and the people who own Hertz
will look very different,

00:15:59.630 --> 00:16:02.300
but, again, remember
there aren't that many

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:04.190
car rental agencies.

00:16:04.190 --> 00:16:06.930
So it still has that brand recognition.

00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:09.590
I think that hurts could
have been turned around

00:16:09.590 --> 00:16:11.170
with different management.

00:16:11.170 --> 00:16:14.420
Management that was really
interested in building

00:16:14.420 --> 00:16:17.160
a solid business and getting rid of some

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:18.940
of that corporate debt that they had,

00:16:18.940 --> 00:16:22.410
because that made them more
vulnerable than anybody else.

00:16:22.410 --> 00:16:23.950
You know, it's astonishing.

00:16:23.950 --> 00:16:25.270
That's the Achilles heel.

00:16:25.270 --> 00:16:28.290
If you look at what Frissora
did and what Icahn did,

00:16:28.290 --> 00:16:29.870
they did very different
things to the company,

00:16:29.870 --> 00:16:31.180
but the common thread with both of them

00:16:31.180 --> 00:16:36.180
is they wanted really good
quarterly results fast.

00:16:36.470 --> 00:16:39.660
There is some, I think,
sort of sense of irony

00:16:39.660 --> 00:16:41.620
that they take on this marketing approach

00:16:41.620 --> 00:16:43.700
that's just running as fast as they can,

00:16:43.700 --> 00:16:46.580
and, by the end, Hertz
has a ton of baggage

00:16:46.580 --> 00:16:48.930
and tried to grow really fast

00:16:48.930 --> 00:16:51.730
through some tactics that
didn't end up serving it well.

